   ## Piotr Polesiuk
 
+  ### *Jak zbudowałem własny procesor nie mając miliona dolarów* (How I built my own processor without a million dollars)
 
-  ### *How I Built My Own Processor Without a Million Dollars*
-
-
-  A processor—the heart of every computer—is usually associated with advanced
-  technology requiring enormous financial resources and specialized knowledge.
-  However, it turns out that a processor is made up of components that operate
-  according to very simple principles.
-
-
-  During this lecture, I will explain how a processor works and what it is made
-  of. I will also show that, with a little creativity and determination, you can
-  build a simple processor yourself without spending a fortune—all thanks to a
-  somewhat forgotten technology from half a century ago.
+  A processor — the heart of every computer — is usually associated with advanced technology requiring enormous financial resources and specialist knowledge. However, it turns out that a processor is made up of components that operate according to very simple principles.
 
+  During this lecture, I will explain how a processor works and what it is made of. I will also show that, with a little creativity and determination, you can build a simple processor yourself without spending a fortune — all thanks to a somewhat forgotten technology from half a century ago.
 
   ## Michał Bałwanowski
 
+  ### *Nieprzewidywalna matematyka prostych reguł* (The unpredictable mathematics of simple rules)
 
-  ### *The Unpredictable Mathematics of Simple Rules*
-
-
-  Can something that follows simple, well-understood rules still surprise us?
-  Absolutely! Sometimes, just a few straightforward rules are enough to generate
-  extraordinary patterns and reveal phenomena whose behavior is difficult to
-  predict.
-
-
-  In this lecture, we will explore mathematics from a slightly different
-  perspective. We will see how simple models can reproduce surprisingly complex
-  phenomena. We will also discover that even seemingly innocent games involving
-  coloring squares can lead to questions that mathematicians are still trying to
-  answer.
+  Can something that follows simple, well-understood rules still surprise us? Absolutely! Sometimes, just a few straightforward rules are enough to generate extraordinary patterns and reveal phenomena whose behaviour is difficult to predict.
 
+  In this lecture, we will explore mathematics from a slightly different perspective. We will see how simple models can reproduce surprisingly complex phenomena. We will also discover that even seemingly innocent games involving colouring squares can lead to questions that mathematicians are still trying to answer.
 
-  There will be plenty of animations, experiments, and opportunities to test
-  your own predictions. The goal is to show that even the simplest mathematics
-  can hold a wealth of surprises.
+  There will be plenty of animations, experiments and opportunities to test your own predictions. The goal is to show that even the simplest mathematics can hold a wealth of surprises.
